{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795284002,"nonce":"o/jQf80NzWVlRQkcn+iNUjyz+Ts=","message":"Fix committed locally as 8188136905f086a19e9923471186d13e61f07c4f (not pushed). Left open until it lands.\n\nWhat it does: hl.Gc.stats() now reports exact bytes and object counts. A read adds up, per registered thread, the objects in its current bump regions (found through the object table) plus bytes retired when a region is replaced or a thread unregisters, plus the locked path and large objects. The allocation fast path is unchanged: the cursor is now an atomic with relaxed load and store. The old ASH_GC_STATS pressure figure is kept for the collector's triggers and the report.\n\nBehaviour to know:\n- Counts start at the first stats read, so only the difference between two reads means anything.\n- Each object counts at its size rounded up to 16 bytes.\n- Only registered mutators are counted. Internal threads, such as LLVM-tier machinery, are not.\n- allocationCount is real now and never runs backwards across a collection.\n\nChecks:\n- Haxe reproducers exact in interp, jit and hybrid, across collections and with the LLVM tier running.\n- ash_std 184, ash_core 231, cross_targets, clippy -D warnings, hybrid conformance 1069/1069, tree_fusion 5/5, the new gc_stats_total test, and the three existing allocation-delta tests.\n- NUC (Linux x86_64, cores 0,2,4,6), interleaved against HEAD, 9 then 21 iterations. No consistent change: binary_trees interp -2.8%, hybrid -3.7%; fasta hybrid -6.8%; knucleotide hybrid -4.4%; closure_call and deltablue +0.3%. Small rows near the startup floor (array_push, gc, jsonparse) moved by under 8% with alternating signs. Tree fusion still elides allocation: 900 MiB to 4 MiB, about 51 ms against 297 ms with it off.\n\nNot done: the diagnostic-overhead benchmark the acceptance asked for. The fast path is unchanged apart from the atomic, so the NUC A/B stands in for it.